    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a fair assessment. I'm curious about the grouping though. You put Marcus, Andre, and Kenji in the \"gritty crime\" bucket, but Kenji is a coder. Do you think his character works better in a crime setting because of the tech aspect, or is there something about his personality profile that just fits that genre more naturally? \n\nAnd being totally honest—since I'm trying to avoid clichés—do you think the fact that my \"street-smart\" characters are Black and my \"high-society\" characters are White/European carries weight here? Like, is that a trope I'm actively using, or is it just how these characters are written?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Kenji fits the gritty genre primarily due to his disciplined, intense personality which mirrors the high-stakes precision of a modern investigator, though his coding skills provide a practical tool for solving crimes without needing traditional exploits.\n\nRegarding the racial groupings, casting a Black protagonist as the \"street-smart\" archetype and White characters in high-society roles can easily replicate stereotypes unless you actively subvert the contexts to make their motivations transcendent of their backgrounds.\n\nTo avoid tropes, consider giving Kenji a non-crime motivation like protecting a vulnerable family member, and ensure the Black street characters have complex motivations that defy the \"everyman criminal\" cliché.",
      "source": "target",
